A link variable can point to the place that leads to the current node. It starts with the address of `head`, so inserting before the first node and inserting in the middle use the same code.

```go
func insertBeforeX(head *ListNode, X int, val int) *ListNode {
    link := &head

    for *link != nil && (*link).Data != X {
        link = &(*link).Next
    }

    if *link != nil {
        inserted := &ListNode{
            Data: val,
            Next: *link,
        }
        *link = inserted
    }

    return head
}
```

Dry run with `10 -> 20 -> 30`, `X = 20`, and `val = 15`:

- `link` first points to `head`, which leads to `10`.
- It moves to the link from `10` to `20`.
- The new node is placed into that link, giving `10 -> 15 -> 20 -> 30`.

For an empty list or a missing target, `*link` is `nil` and the function returns the original head. The input list is updated in place. The scan takes `O(n)` time and uses `O(1)` extra space.

The head needs special care because `X` may already be the first value. This version handles that case first, then keeps a pointer to the node before the possible match.

If the target is missing, no new node is added.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode insertBeforeX(ListNode head, int X, int val) {
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        if (head.data == X) {
            return new ListNode(val, head);
        }

        ListNode previous = head;
        while (previous.next != null && previous.next.data != X) {
            previous = previous.next;
        }

        if (previous.next != null) {
            previous.next = new ListNode(val, previous.next);
        }

        return head;
    }
}
```

An empty list returns `null`. A one-node list also works: it either gets a new head when its value is `X`, or remains the same when it is not.

This changes the existing links but does not copy the list. Time complexity is `O(n)`, with `O(1)` extra space.

This keeps the head case separate, then checks the value after the current node. That means `current` always acts as the predecessor of the node where the insertion may happen.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public ListNode InsertBeforeX(ListNode head, int X, int val) {
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        if (head.data == X) {
            return new ListNode(val, head);
        }

        ListNode current = head;
        while (current.next != null && current.next.data != X) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        if (current.next != null) {
            ListNode inserted = new ListNode(val);
            inserted.next = current.next;
            current.next = inserted;
        }

        return head;
    }
}
```

The loop stops at the last node if there is no match, so the extra `if` prevents an invalid link update. The original list is changed only when `X` is found.

Time complexity is `O(n)`. The method uses `O(1)` extra space.

A dummy node makes the first-position case easy. Start with `prev` at the dummy, move until `prev.next` contains `X`, then place the new node between `prev` and `prev.next`. If no node has the target value, the list stays unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* insertBeforeX(ListNode* &head, int X, int val) {
        ListNode dummy(0, head);
        ListNode* previous = &dummy;

        while (previous->next != nullptr && previous->next->data != X) {
            previous = previous->next;
        }

        if (previous->next != nullptr) {
            ListNode* inserted = new ListNode(val, previous->next);
            previous->next = inserted;
        }

        head = dummy.next;
        return head;
    }
};
```

For example, with `4 -> 7 -> 9` and `X = 7`, the scan stops at node `4`. The links become `4 -> val -> 7 -> 9`.

The input list is changed by adding one node. The time complexity is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(1)`.

